// ── THE LIVE READER PROBE (B2c of the approval-free branch boot plan) ─────────────────────────────
//
// WHAT THIS READS, AND WHY ANYTHING HAS TO. The boot medium reader key (gunbc.host
// boot_medium_reader_key) is restricted and forced: one authorized_keys line, installed by B2b
// (gunbc.host.export_host_reader_converge), admitting exactly one command -- sha256sum of the served
// image -- from one source address. The lane's RED is one sentence: any command OTHER than the forced
// one, presented under the reader key, must be answered with ONLY the forced sha256sum line. That
// sentence is the entire reason PR-C may run a branch boot without an approval: the branch cell keeps
// this key and loses the fleet key, so the account's last privilege must be one digest -- and a last
// privilege is only known by being read. A ForceCommand sshd ignores, a key file the account can
// rewrite, an authorized_keys the account can append to: every one of them says the right thing on disk,
// and none of them is visible to a plan that only reads the files B2b wrote.
//
// WHY A PROBE AND NOT A PROOF. `sshd -T` renders the effective configuration, and B2b already reads it
// for AuthorizedKeysFile. It does not render what a forced command DOES, because that is not a
// configuration value; it is behavior. Behavior is knowable only by authenticating and asking.
//
// THE TWO OPEN FACTS, READ RATHER THAN INFERRED (B2b's body names both as B2c's own):
//   1. THE LOCKED `--system` ACCOUNT AND UsePAM. The reader account is created by
//      executor_privileged_operation EnsureSystemAccount, which runs `useradd --system`, so its password
//      field is locked. Whether sshd admits that account's publickey login anyway turns on UsePAM: with
//      `UsePAM yes` (Ubuntu's default) OpenSSH skips the locked-account refusal. That is a reading of
//      OpenSSH's behaviour, not a fact about this host, and the reader's login is the ground PR-C stands
//      on -- so all three parts are read here: the shadow field (is it actually locked), the EFFECTIVE
//      `usepam` value (`sshd -T`, not a config file: the drop-in and the base config both contribute, and
//      only sshd's own rendering says what the running daemon will do), and the login itself. Three
//      readings that agree is a fact; one of them inferred is not.
//   2. CAN IT READ /srv/bmc. `boot_image_export_ownership` declares no other-read on the export, so on
//      the face of the model the reader cannot traverse it. Whether the served link is readable by that
//      account is read two ways, because the two mechanisms can disagree and a disagreement is itself a
//      finding: from the ADMINISTRATOR leg as that account (`sudo -u <reader> test -r <link>`), and from
//      the forced command's own exit code -- a forced command that authenticated and then failed to read
//      its file is that same fact seen from inside the account. It is never asked of the reader key as a
//      question, because under a forced command the reader's answer to ANY question is the digest line.
//
// WHAT REDS, each stage named so a run says which fact failed:
//   key        the public row is not pinned, or carries no fingerprint: the mint has not landed, so there
//              is no reader key and nothing else here can mean anything;
//   agent      the run's agent does not hold the PINNED reader key. This is the observable consequence of
//              the accessor grant being live: the private half can only be read out of Secret Manager by
//              a principal the read is granted to (gunbc.auth.fleet_secret_accessor_roster
//              boot_medium_reader_key_accessor_row), and only a read puts its fingerprint in the agent. So
//              an agent without this fingerprint means the read did not happen -- and it refuses rather
//              than proceeding, because every reader leg would otherwise authenticate with the FLEET key
//              and report the fleet account's privileges as the reader's: the one result that could let
//              PR-C through falsely;
//   target     srv2 could not be resolved or anchored;
//   transport  the reader leg never reached a daemon that answered (port down, endpoint wrong, our own
//              known_hosts row stale). Named separately from `reader` on purpose: exit 255 looks identical
//              for both, only ONE of them is an authentication finding, and the fix for a transport failure
//              is never a PAM change;
//   reader     the account refused the pinned key -- open fact 1 reading NO, the one finding PR-C cannot
//              be built over;
//   red        the second command under the reader key was answered with anything other than the forced
//              line, compared on exit code, stdout AND stderr -- the lane's own RED;
//   facts      a fact could not be read at all, or the three readings of fact 1 contradict. A missing
//              reading is a refusal, never a default: a run that could not read something does not get to
//              report it.
//
// WHAT IS RECORDED, NOT ASSERTED. Open fact 2's POLARITY: B2b deliberately declared no other-read, so
// "unreadable" is that ruling working and "readable" means something unnamed is granting access. Both
// answers are admissible and the receipt says which, because PR-C's BMC-only cell is built on top of the
// answer. Likewise the digest: agreeing with the medium row is the boot's own readback's business
// (boot_medium_served_readback_over_reader), not this probe's.
//
// WHY THE ACCESSOR IS NOT PROBED WITH testIamPermissions. extdeps.cloud.gcp.secret_manager models
// TestSecretIamPermissions as DIAGNOSTIC ONLY and deliberately has no "absent" constructor: it returns the
// empty set for a resource that does not exist, answers only about the permissions handed to it, and is
// documented as potentially permissive. Treating "the permission was not in the list" as a refused grant
// would be concluding absence from the one instrument this repository has ruled out for that question. So
// the grant is checked where it is unmissable -- an agent cannot hold a fingerprint whose key nobody could
// have read -- and the policy is REPORTED, with its own limits stated, not judged.
//
// NO KEY MATERIAL IN THE RUNTIME, LET ALONE THE RECEIPT. No value in this module can hold a private half:
// there is no secret read here at all. That is B1's rule (gunbc.host.boot_medium_reader_key_mint) honored
// structurally rather than by redaction discipline -- a type that cannot carry a key needs no redaction to
// keep it out of a log. Both commands asked under the reader key are read-only and print nothing secret
// even in the case where the server ran one, which is the case the RED exists to catch. The quoted answers
// are forced-command output by construction, and the wording still says so, because a diagnostic path whose
// safety depends on reasoning about what a far side could echo is the path that leaks.

data probe_attempt_raw: String = "boot-medium-reader-probe"

// SPOKEN OF THE KEY WHEN THERE IS NO KEY. The fold refuses at StageKey before it reads this, so it never
// reaches a receipt as a fingerprint would; the string says so in its own text rather than looking like the
// SHA256:<43 base64> shape a real one has.
data probe_no_fingerprint: NonEmptyStr = "no-key: the reader keypair is not pinned"

data boot_medium_reader_probe_receipt_path: String = "target/boot-medium-reader-probe.txt"

// THE SHADOW FIELD THAT MEANS LOCKED: `!`, `*`, or `*LK*` in field two of `getent shadow <login>`
// (shadow(5): a leading `!` marks a password that cannot be used for authentication). An empty or hashed
// field is NOT locked, so the reader's admission is reported against what the field actually holds rather
// than against what `useradd --system` was expected to do.
fn reader_shadow_field_is_locked(shadow_line: String, login: NonEmptyStr) -> Bool {
  let fields = split(s: shadow_line, delimiter: ":")
  match fields.first() {
    Present { value: name } =>
      if name != (login as String) {
        false
      } else {
        match get(xs: fields, index: 1) {
          // THE LOCK MARKERS ONLY. `passwd -l`/`usermod -L` prefix the hash with `!`, and some tools write
          // `*LK*`; those are the lock. A bare `*` is NOT a lock -- it is "no UNIX password", the state a
          // --system account is created with -- and `""` is "no password at all"; reporting either as locked
          // would make the open fact read "locked" on every host and the probe would stop being a witness.
          // The distinction is load-bearing exactly where the three readings meet: under UsePAM=no, a `!`
          // field that still authenticated is a contradiction, while a `*` field that authenticated is
          // ordinary PAM behavior.
          Present { value: f } => starts_with(s: f, prefix: "!") || starts_with(s: f, prefix: "*LK*")
          Absent => false
        }
      }
    Absent => false
  }
}

// THE TWO REFUSALS THAT LOOK IDENTICAL AND ARE NOT. Exit 255 is what ssh(1) returns for "the account would
// not have me" AND for "I never reached a daemon that would answer": one is open fact 1's answer and the
// other is a fact about the network or about our own trust anchor. Collapsing them would let a stale
// known_hosts row or a down port be reported as "the locked account cannot log in" -- which is the single
// most expensive wrong conclusion available to this lane, because the tempting fix for a PAM finding
// (unlock the account, or flip UsePAM) does nothing for a transport failure and actively harms the posture
// the account exists to have. So the phrases are split, and only the authentication set may reach the
// PAM-shaped stage.
//
// THE ssh PHRASE IS MATCHED FULLY QUALIFIED, as `Permission denied (publickey`, and NOT as the bare
// "Permission denied" -- because the failure this probe is built to observe is a forced `sha256sum` that
// cannot open the served image, and coreutils prints exactly `sha256sum: <path>: Permission denied` for
// that. A looser match would read open fact 2's own evidence as open fact 1's answer and report "the locked
// account cannot log in" on a host where the account logged in perfectly and could not read the file.
type ReaderProbeRefusal
  = ProbeNotRefused
  | ProbeAuthenticationRefused
  | ProbeTransportRefused

fn reader_probe_refusal_class(a: RemoteAnswer) -> ReaderProbeRefusal {
  let e = trim(s: a.stderr)
  if a.exit_code == 0 {
    ProbeNotRefused
  } else if e.contains("Host key verification failed") || e.contains("Connection refused") || e.contains("Connection timed out") || e.contains("No route to host") || e.contains("Name or service not known") {
    ProbeTransportRefused
  } else if e.contains("Permission denied (publickey") || e.contains("no supported authentication methods") || e.contains("Too many authentication failures") {
    ProbeAuthenticationRefused
  } else {
    ProbeNotRefused
  }
}

fn reader_probe_answer_text(a: RemoteAnswer) -> String {
  join(["exit=", to_string(a.exit_code), " stdout=[", trim(s: a.stdout), "] stderr=[", trim(s: a.stderr), "]"], "")
}

// ── THE FORCED ANSWER AND THE RED ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The line's shape is the one the served readback already admits (boot_medium_served_reader_join):
// `<64 hex>  <link>`, exit 0, nothing else. Two readers of one forced command must not be able to
// disagree about what it means, so the shape is judged by that same classifier and the same spelling of
// the line, not a second one invented here.
type ReaderProbeForcedAnswer
  = ForcedAnswerHeld { digest: NonEmptyStr }
  | ForcedAnswerImageUnreadable { evidence: NonEmptyStr }
  | ForcedAnswerMalformed { evidence: NonEmptyStr }
  | ForcedAnswerLoginRefused { evidence: NonEmptyStr }
  | ForcedAnswerTransportBlocked { evidence: NonEmptyStr }
  | ForcedAnswerUnexplained { evidence: NonEmptyStr }

// CLASSIFY THE FORCED LEG; the order IS the meaning, and the LAST arm is the one that makes this honest.
//   transport -- nothing reached an authentication decision, so NO fact is being answered here and the run
//     must not be allowed to imply one. It is refused under its own stage, not the reader-login one;
//   authentication -- open fact 1 reading NO, the only finding PR-C cannot be built over;
//   non-zero WITH a stderr -- a command that authenticated, ran, and reported why it failed: open fact 2
//     from the only side that can see it from inside the account. Requiring non-empty stderr matters: a
//     silent non-zero is not a command reporting a read failure, it is an absence of information, and
//     reading silence as "the image is unreadable" would be exactly the invented fact this probe exists to
//     prevent;
//   zero exit, line not the digest -- the forced command not holding, which is the RED's business and not
//     this classifier's;
//   anything else -- named, and refused. The fold has no default arm anywhere, so a new shape of answer can
//     only ever arrive as one of these names.
fn reader_probe_forced_answer(a: RemoteAnswer, link: NonEmptyStr) -> ReaderProbeForcedAnswer {
  let line = trim(s: a.stdout)
  match reader_probe_refusal_class(a: a) {
    ProbeTransportRefused => ForcedAnswerTransportBlocked { evidence: reader_probe_answer_text(a: a) as NonEmptyStr }
    ProbeAuthenticationRefused => ForcedAnswerLoginRefused { evidence: reader_probe_answer_text(a: a) as NonEmptyStr }
    ProbeNotRefused =>
      if a.exit_code != 0 && trim(s: a.stderr) != "" {
        ForcedAnswerImageUnreadable { evidence: reader_probe_answer_text(a: a) as NonEmptyStr }
      } else if a.exit_code != 0 {
        ForcedAnswerUnexplained { evidence: reader_probe_answer_text(a: a) as NonEmptyStr }
      } else {
        match sha256sum_digest(line: line) {
          Absent => ForcedAnswerMalformed { evidence: reader_probe_answer_text(a: a) as NonEmptyStr }
          Present { value: d } =>
            if line != join([d, "  ", link as String], "") {
              ForcedAnswerMalformed { evidence: reader_probe_answer_text(a: a) as NonEmptyStr }
            } else {
              ForcedAnswerHeld { digest: d as NonEmptyStr }
            }
        }
      }
  }
}

// THE RED ITSELF, pure over the two answers. Equality on ALL THREE fields, not stdout alone: a server that
// ran the second command and printed its own output while also printing the digest would be a server whose
// forced command is not holding, and a comparison that looked only at stdout would pass it.
fn reader_probe_forced_command_masks(forced: RemoteAnswer, other: RemoteAnswer) -> Bool {
  other.exit_code == forced.exit_code && other.stdout == forced.stdout && other.stderr == forced.stderr
}

fn reader_probe_red_cause(forced: RemoteAnswer, other: RemoteAnswer) -> String {
  join([
    "the reader key's forced command did not hold: a second command was answered differently from the forced one. ",
    "forced: ", reader_probe_answer_text(a: forced), " | second: ", reader_probe_answer_text(a: other), ". ",
    "Under the reader key the account must be able to run nothing but the served image's digest.",
  ], "")
}

// ── THE TWO OPEN FACTS, AS DATA ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
type ReaderProbeLockedAccountFact
  = LockedAccountRead { shadow_locked: Bool, use_pam: NonEmptyStr, login_admitted: Bool }
  | LockedAccountUnreadable { cause: NonEmptyStr }

// THE FACT IS READ, NOT ASSUMED, and the combination that cannot be true is named rather than smoothed
// over: a login admitted while the shadow field is locked AND sshd reports usepam=no is a reading that
// cannot be believed -- either the daemon answering is not the one that rendered this config, or something
// other than sshd admitted the key. The lane stops on that instead of building PR-C on it.
fn reader_probe_locked_account_fact(shadow: RemoteAnswer, use_pam: RemoteAnswer, login_admitted: Bool, login: NonEmptyStr) -> ReaderProbeLockedAccountFact {
  if shadow.exit_code != 0 {
    LockedAccountUnreadable { cause: join(["the shadow entry could not be read: ", reader_probe_answer_text(a: shadow)], "") as NonEmptyStr }
  } else if use_pam.exit_code != 0 {
    LockedAccountUnreadable { cause: join(["sshd -T refused: ", reader_probe_answer_text(a: use_pam)], "") as NonEmptyStr }
  } else {
    match sshd_effective_config_value(text: use_pam.stdout, keyword: "usepam") {
      Absent => LockedAccountUnreadable { cause: "sshd -T rendered no usepam keyword" as NonEmptyStr }
      Present { value: v } => {
        let pam = trim(s: v)
        let locked = reader_shadow_field_is_locked(shadow_line: trim(s: shadow.stdout), login: login)
        if login_admitted && locked && (pam == "no" || pam == "0") {
          LockedAccountUnreadable {
            cause: join([
              "the three readings contradict each other and none can be trusted: the shadow field is locked, sshd -T reports ",
              "usepam=", pam, ", and yet the reader account authenticated. Either the daemon answering is not the one that ",
              "rendered this config, or something other than sshd admitted the key. Fix the reading before building on it.",
            ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
          }
        } else {
          LockedAccountRead { shadow_locked: locked, use_pam: pam as NonEmptyStr, login_admitted: login_admitted }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

// OPEN FACT 2, as a record rather than a Tuple: this language has no product type, and the two witnesses
// are kept as two named fields because a receipt that collapsed them into one bool would hide the case
// where they disagree -- which is the interesting one.
type ReaderProbeExportReading = ReaderProbeExportReading {
  readable: Bool,
  admin_evidence: NonEmptyStr,
  forced_evidence: NonEmptyStr,
}

// THE ADMIN-WITNESS OF FACT 2. The polarity is evidence and never an assertion; see the header for why
// B2b's own ownership ruling makes both answers admissible.
fn reader_probe_export_reading(admin: RemoteAnswer, forced: ReaderProbeForcedAnswer) -> ReaderProbeExportReading {
  let forced_says = match forced {
    ForcedAnswerHeld { digest: _ } => "the forced command read the served image (its digest line was admitted)"
    ForcedAnswerImageUnreadable { evidence: e } => join(["the forced command authenticated but could not read the served image: ", e as String], "")
    ForcedAnswerMalformed { evidence: e } => join(["the forced command answered with a malformed line: ", e as String], "")
    ForcedAnswerLoginRefused { evidence: e } => join(["the reader never authenticated, so nothing was read: ", e as String], "")
    ForcedAnswerTransportBlocked { evidence: e } => join(["the reader never reached the daemon, so nothing was read: ", e as String], "")
    ForcedAnswerUnexplained { evidence: e } => join(["the forced command failed without explaining itself: ", e as String], "")
  }
  ReaderProbeExportReading {
    readable: admin.exit_code == 0,
    admin_evidence: join(["admin leg asked as the reader account: ", reader_probe_answer_text(a: admin)], "") as NonEmptyStr,
    forced_evidence: forced_says as NonEmptyStr,
  }
}

// ── THE ACCESSOR, REPORTED WITHIN ITS INSTRUMENT'S LIMITS ─────────────────────────────────────────
// The permission the accessor cell alone confers, and a RECORDING of what the service says the run holds.
// Recording rather than judging is the design of this section: see the header for why absence cannot be
// concluded from this instrument, and the agent stage for the check that does not need to.
data accessor_permission: String = "secretmanager.googleapis.com/versions.access"

type ReaderProbeAccessorReport
  = AccessorReportUnreadable { reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | AccessorReport { permission_listed: Bool, resource: NonEmptyStr }

fn reader_probe_accessor_report() -> ReaderProbeAccessorReport {
  match ensure_access_token(source: WorkloadIdentityToken) {
    AccessTokenReady { token } =>
      match gcp.SecretManager.TestSecretIamPermissions(
        access_token: token,
        project_id: fleet_secrets_project_id,
        secret: boot_medium_reader_ssh_privkey_secret.secret,
        permissions: [accessor_permission],
      ) {
        RestAnswered { answer: r } =>
          AccessorReport {
            permission_listed: any(match r.permissions { Present { value: ps } => ps Absent => [] }, p => p == accessor_permission),
            resource: boot_medium_reader_ssh_privkey_secret.secret,
          }
        RestRefused { refusal: _ } => AccessorReportUnreadable { reason: "testIamPermissions refused" as NonEmptyStr }
      }
    AccessTokenUpsertRequired { plan: _ } => AccessorReportUnreadable { reason: "no Secret Manager access token: access upsert required" as NonEmptyStr }
    AccessTokenEnsureRefused { reason } => AccessorReportUnreadable { reason: reason as NonEmptyStr }
  }
}

// ── THE RECORD ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
type ReaderProbeStage
  = StageKey
  | StageAgent
  | StageTarget
  | StageTransport
  | StageReaderLogin
  | StageRed
  | StageUnexplained
  | StageFacts

// The forced command's own reading, kept as a record so "the image is not readable" is a REPORTED state
// rather than a silently-absent digest: it is open fact 2 from inside the account, and PR-C must be built
// knowing which of the two it is.
type ForcedLineRecord
  = ForcedLineHeld { digest: NonEmptyStr }
  | ForcedLineImageUnreadable { evidence: NonEmptyStr }

type ReaderProbeReceipt
  = ReaderProbeObserved {
      fingerprint: NonEmptyStr,
      forced: ForcedLineRecord,
      second_command_masked: Bool,
      locked_account: ReaderProbeLockedAccountFact,
      export: ReaderProbeExportReading,
      accessor: ReaderProbeAccessorReport,
    }
  | ReaderProbeRefused { stage: ReaderProbeStage, cause: NonEmptyStr }

fn reader_probe_stage_text(stage: ReaderProbeStage) -> String {
  match stage {
    StageKey => "key"
    StageAgent => "agent"
    StageTarget => "target"
    StageTransport => "transport"
    StageReaderLogin => "reader"
    StageRed => "red"
    StageUnexplained => "unexplained"
    StageFacts => "facts"
  }
}

// THE PROBE AS ONE FOLD, so every leg's answer can be supplied by a witness and the gate is testable
// without a host. The order is the gate, and it is not rearrangeable: no leg is issued until the agent is
// proven to hold the pinned key and srv2 is anchored, and the RED is only meaningful once the forced
// command has answered -- a second command returning the same error as the first proves nothing about a
// forced command that never ran.
fn boot_medium_reader_probe_fold(
  pinned: Bool,
  agent_holds_pinned_key: Bool,
  fingerprint: NonEmptyStr,
  target_reached: Bool,
  target_cause: String,
  forced: RemoteAnswer,
  second: RemoteAnswer,
  shadow: RemoteAnswer,
  use_pam: RemoteAnswer,
  admin_readable: RemoteAnswer,
  login: NonEmptyStr,
  accessor: ReaderProbeAccessorReport,
) -> ReaderProbeReceipt {
  let link = boot_medium_served_link_path()
  if pinned == false {
    ReaderProbeRefused { stage: StageKey, cause: "the reader keypair is not pinned (boot_medium_reader_ssh_key is none, or carries no fingerprint): there is no reader key to probe. Run the mint (gunbc.host.boot_medium_reader_key_mint) under the operator's approval first." as NonEmptyStr }
  } else if agent_holds_pinned_key == false {
    ReaderProbeRefused {
      stage: StageAgent,
      cause: join([
        "the run's agent does not hold the pinned reader key ", fingerprint as String, ". The private half is read out of Secret Manager by ",
        "the modeled load step, and that read is granted by exactly one row -- boot_medium_reader_key_accessor_row ",
        "(gunbc.auth.fleet_secret_accessor_roster). The mint's adder row cannot make this fingerprint appear, because it can write ",
        "a version and cannot read one back. Proceeding would authenticate every reader leg with the FLEET key and report the ",
        "fleet account's privileges as the reader's, which is the one result that could let PR-C through falsely.",
      ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
    }
  } else if target_reached == false {
    ReaderProbeRefused { stage: StageTarget, cause: target_cause }
  } else {
    match reader_probe_forced_answer(a: forced, link: link) {
      ForcedAnswerLoginRefused { evidence: e } =>
        // Open fact 1, read as NO: the finding PR-C cannot be built over, so refuse by name and say what to
        // look at -- including the thing NOT to do, because unlocking the account would turn a red run green
        // by destroying the property the account exists to have.
        ReaderProbeRefused {
          stage: StageReaderLogin,
          cause: join([
            "the reader account refused the pinned key: ", e as String,
            ". This is open fact 1 answering NO -- a locked `--system` account's publickey login depends on UsePAM, and PR-C's ",
            "BMC-only branch cell depends on this login. Read the usepam and shadow readings before assuming a cause, and do NOT ",
            "work around it by unlocking the account: a usable password on a key-only service account is a worse posture than the ",
            "boot this probe exists to approve.",
          ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
        }
      ForcedAnswerTransportBlocked { evidence: e } =>
        // NOT a fact about the host, and deliberately not reported as one. Nothing reached an authentication
        // decision, so neither open fact was answered and this run must not imply that they were. The advice
        // names the reading to fix (reachability and our own trust anchor) and says plainly that PAM is not
        // implicated, because the expensive mistake at this stage is "the locked account cannot log in, let me
        // unlock it" -- a change that would do nothing here and would remove the property the account has.
        ReaderProbeRefused { stage: StageTransport, cause: join(["srv2 did not answer the reader leg: ", e as String, ". This is a reachability or trust-anchor reading failure, NOT an authentication finding: neither open fact was answered, and UsePAM is not implicated. Check the endpoint, the port, and the known_hosts row before anything else."], "") as NonEmptyStr }
      ForcedAnswerUnexplained { evidence: e } =>
        // A non-zero exit with nothing on stderr is not a command reporting a read failure; it is an absence
        // of information, and inventing "the image is unreadable" from silence would put a manufactured fact
        // into the receipt PR-C is built on. So the shape is named and refused until it can be explained.
        ReaderProbeRefused { stage: StageUnexplained, cause: join(["the forced command failed without explaining itself: ", e as String, ". A non-zero exit with no stderr is not evidence that the served image is unreadable -- it is the absence of the reading that would decide open fact 2."], "") as NonEmptyStr }
      ForcedAnswerMalformed { evidence: e } =>
        ReaderProbeRefused { stage: StageRed, cause: join(["the forced command did not answer with its own line: ", e as String], "") as NonEmptyStr }
      ForcedAnswerHeld { digest: d } =>
        reader_probe_after_masking(fingerprint: fingerprint, forced_record: ForcedLineHeld { digest: d }, forced: forced, second: second, shadow: shadow, use_pam: use_pam, admin_readable: admin_readable, login: login, accessor: accessor)
      ForcedAnswerImageUnreadable { evidence: e } =>
        // The forced command DID authenticate and answer, so the masking test is still meaningful: the second
        // command must be answered with this same line. Only the digest is missing, and that is open fact 2.
        reader_probe_after_masking(fingerprint: fingerprint, forced_record: ForcedLineImageUnreadable { evidence: e }, forced: forced, second: second, shadow: shadow, use_pam: use_pam, admin_readable: admin_readable, login: login, accessor: accessor)
    }
  }
}

// THE RED, THEN THE FACTS -- one function, because the order is itself a claim: the masking test is judged
// first, so a run that failed the RED cannot also present its fact readings as if they were the finding.
fn reader_probe_after_masking(
  fingerprint: NonEmptyStr,
  forced_record: ForcedLineRecord,
  forced: RemoteAnswer,
  second: RemoteAnswer,
  shadow: RemoteAnswer,
  use_pam: RemoteAnswer,
  admin_readable: RemoteAnswer,
  login: NonEmptyStr,
  accessor: ReaderProbeAccessorReport,
) -> ReaderProbeReceipt {
  if reader_probe_forced_command_masks(forced: forced, other: second) == false {
    ReaderProbeRefused { stage: StageRed, cause: reader_probe_red_cause(forced: forced, other: second) as NonEmptyStr }
  } else {
    // The login is admitted on the evidence that a command RAN under this key: an authentication refusal was
    // already classified out of the forced leg, so reaching here means the server accepted the key and ran
    // the forced command. It is deliberately not inferred from a zero exit code, which is also what a forced
    // command that failed to read its file returns -- that would report a fact 1 of NO as a fact 1 of YES.
    let fact = reader_probe_locked_account_fact(shadow: shadow, use_pam: use_pam, login_admitted: true, login: login)
    match fact {
      LockedAccountUnreadable { cause: c } => ReaderProbeRefused { stage: StageFacts, cause: c }
      LockedAccountRead { shadow_locked: _, use_pam: _, login_admitted: _ } =>
        ReaderProbeObserved {
          fingerprint: fingerprint,
          forced: forced_record,
          second_command_masked: true,
          locked_account: fact,
          export: reader_probe_export_reading(admin: admin_readable, forced: reader_probe_forced_answer(a: forced, link: boot_medium_served_link_path())),
          accessor: accessor,
        }
    }
  }
}

// THE RECEIPT. Keys are excluded by construction rather than redacted: no type here holds a private half,
// so no arm can render one, and the fingerprint is the only thing ever said about the key.
fn render_reader_probe_receipt(r: ReaderProbeReceipt) -> String {
  match r {
    ReaderProbeObserved { fingerprint: f, forced: fl, second_command_masked: masked, locked_account: la, export: ex, accessor: acc } =>
      join([
        "reader_probe=observed\n",
        "fingerprint=", f as String, "\n",
        "second_command_masked=", if masked { "true" } else { "false" }, "\n",
        render_forced_line(fl: fl),
        reader_probe_locked_account_text(fact: la),
        "image_readable_by_reader=", if ex.readable { "true" } else { "false" }, "\n",
        "image_read_admin_evidence=", ex.admin_evidence as String, "\n",
        "image_read_forced_evidence=", ex.forced_evidence as String, "\n",
        render_accessor_report(acc: acc),
      ], "")
    ReaderProbeRefused { stage: s, cause: c } => join(["reader_probe=refused\nstage=", reader_probe_stage_text(stage: s), "\nreason=", c as String, "\n"], "")
  }
}

fn render_forced_line(fl: ForcedLineRecord) -> String {
  match fl {
    ForcedLineHeld { digest: d } => join(["forced_digest=", d as String, "\n"], "")
    ForcedLineImageUnreadable { evidence: e } => join(["forced_digest=unreadable\nforced_evidence=", e as String, "\n"], "")
  }
}

fn reader_probe_locked_account_text(fact: ReaderProbeLockedAccountFact) -> String {
  match fact {
    LockedAccountRead { shadow_locked: l, use_pam: u, login_admitted: a } =>
      join([
        "shadow_password_field_locked=", if l { "true" } else { "false" }, "\n",
        "sshd_effective_usepam=", u as String, "\n",
        "locked_system_account_login_admitted=", if a { "true" } else { "false" }, "\n",
      ], "")
    LockedAccountUnreadable { cause: c } => join(["locked_account_fact=unreadable reason=", c as String, "\n"], "")
  }
}

// The accessor line states what the instrument answered AND what it cannot answer, so a reader of the log
// cannot mistake `permission_listed=false` for a refusal of the grant.
fn render_accessor_report(acc: ReaderProbeAccessorReport) -> String {
  match acc {
    AccessorReportUnreadable { reason } => join(["accessor_probe=unreadable reason=", reason as String, "\n"], "")
    AccessorReport { permission_listed: p, resource: res } =>
      join([
        "accessor_probe=reported resource=", res as String, " versions_access_listed=", if p { "true" } else { "false" },
        " (testIamPermissions is diagnostic only: an unlisted permission here is not a refusal of the grant, and the grant is ",
        "enforced by the agent stage above)\n",
      ], "")
  }
}

// THE GATE THE STEP CHECKS. An observation whose forced command could not read the image is still an
// observation -- that is open fact 2's answer, recorded -- so the hold does not require a digest.
fn reader_probe_holds(r: ReaderProbeReceipt) -> Bool {
  match r {
    ReaderProbeObserved { fingerprint: _, forced: _, second_command_masked: masked, locked_account: _, export: _, accessor: _ } => masked
    ReaderProbeRefused { stage: _, cause: _ } => false
  }
}

// ── THE WET ENTRY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `gunbc run --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry dag/gunbc/host/boot_medium_reader_probe.dag --function boot_medium_reader_probe_wet`
// CONSUMPTION (DESIGN section 3c): invoked by the fleet-converge mode BootMediumReaderProbe
// (gunbc.fleet.fleet_converge_workflow), on the self-hosted runner under the fleet identity at main, after
// the modeled agent-load step has put the reader key in the run's agent. srv2 is named here and is never a
// dispatch input; the receipt is written whatever the outcome, so a refusal is evidence rather than a red
// step with no text.
fn boot_medium_reader_probe_wet() -> ProcessExit {
  let accessor = reader_probe_accessor_report()
  match boot_medium_reader_ssh_key {
    Absent => boot_medium_reader_probe_write(boot_medium_reader_probe_fold(
      pinned: false, agent_holds_pinned_key: false, fingerprint: probe_no_fingerprint,
      target_reached: false, target_cause: "",
      forced: unreadable_answer(), second: unreadable_answer(), shadow: unreadable_answer(), use_pam: unreadable_answer(), admin_readable: unreadable_answer(),
      login: boot_medium_reader_login, accessor: accessor,
    ))
    Present { value: key } =>
      match boot_medium_reader_ssh_key_fingerprint {
        Absent => boot_medium_reader_probe_write(ReaderProbeRefused { stage: StageKey, cause: "the reader key carries no recorded fingerprint (gunbc.host.boot_medium_reader_key boot_medium_reader_ssh_key_fingerprint), so the agent can never be proven to hold THIS key rather than some other" as NonEmptyStr })
        Present { value: fingerprint } =>
          // The agent's contents are verified before any leg, by the same preparation every other route
          // uses: it materializes this key's PUBLIC selector and requires the agent to hold this key and no
          // other reading of it. That is the whole agent stage, and it is what makes the load step
          // non-vacuous. Because the transport that preparation produces passes -o IdentitiesOnly=yes with
          // this selector as its only IdentityFile, the administrator legs below cannot be answered by the
          // reader key and vice versa: one run can hold two keys and still mean two different things by them.
          match prepare_ssh_agent_context(attempt_raw: probe_attempt_raw, key: key, fingerprint: fingerprint, rows: fleet_known_hosts_rows()) {
            FleetSshContextRefused { cause: _ } => boot_medium_reader_probe_write(boot_medium_reader_probe_fold(
              pinned: true, agent_holds_pinned_key: false, fingerprint: fingerprint,
              target_reached: false, target_cause: "",
              forced: unreadable_answer(), second: unreadable_answer(), shadow: unreadable_answer(), use_pam: unreadable_answer(), admin_readable: unreadable_answer(),
              login: boot_medium_reader_login, accessor: accessor,
            ))
            FleetSshContextReady { context: reader_context, receipt: _ } =>
              match fleet_ssh_target_at_standard_port(endpoint: fleet_probe_endpoint_for(host: operator_host_srv2 as String), principal: boot_medium_reader_login) {
                SshTargetRefused { endpoint: e, refusal: _ } => boot_medium_reader_probe_write(ReaderProbeRefused { stage: StageTarget, cause: join(["srv2 could not be resolved as a target for the reader account from endpoint ", e], "") as NonEmptyStr })
                SshTargetResolved { target: reader_target } =>
                  match prepare_fleet_ssh_agent_context(attempt_raw: probe_attempt_raw) {
                    FleetSshContextRefused { cause: c } => boot_medium_reader_probe_write(ReaderProbeRefused { stage: StageAgent, cause: join(["the fleet administrator context could not be prepared, so neither open fact can be read: ", c], "") as NonEmptyStr })
                    FleetSshContextReady { context: admin_context, receipt: _ } =>
                      match fleet_ssh_target_at_standard_port(endpoint: fleet_probe_endpoint_for(host: operator_host_srv2 as String), principal: executor_bootstrap_principal.login) {
                        SshTargetRefused { endpoint: e, refusal: _ } => boot_medium_reader_probe_write(ReaderProbeRefused { stage: StageTarget, cause: join(["srv2 could not be resolved as an administrator target from endpoint ", e], "") as NonEmptyStr })
                        SshTargetResolved { target: admin_target } => {
                          // The facts the reader key cannot be asked about are read from the route that is not
                          // masked; the two reader legs are asked of the key itself. Same host, same port,
                          // same principal name in the audit trail -- different credential, which is the whole
                          // experiment.
                          let forced = reader_leg(target: reader_target, context: reader_context, argv: reader_probe_forced_argv())
                          let second = reader_leg(target: reader_target, context: reader_context, argv: reader_probe_second_command())
                          let shadow = elevated_leg(context: admin_context, target: admin_target, command: argv_words(command: getent_shadow_command(key: boot_medium_reader_login)))
                          let use_pam = elevated_leg(context: admin_context, target: admin_target, command: sshd_effective_config_argv())
                          let admin_readable = as_reader_leg(context: admin_context, target: admin_target)
                          boot_medium_reader_probe_write(boot_medium_reader_probe_fold(
                            pinned: true, agent_holds_pinned_key: true, fingerprint: fingerprint,
                            target_reached: true, target_cause: "",
                            forced: forced, second: second, shadow: shadow, use_pam: use_pam, admin_readable: admin_readable,
                            login: boot_medium_reader_login, accessor: accessor,
                          ))
                        }
                      }
                  }
              }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn boot_medium_reader_probe_write(r: ReaderProbeReceipt) -> ProcessExit {
  let rendered = render_reader_probe_receipt(r: r)
  let written = Filesystem.Write(path: boot_medium_reader_probe_receipt_path, content: rendered)
  if written.success == false {
    exit_failure(reason: concat("boot medium reader probe: the receipt could not be written: ", rendered))
  } else if reader_probe_holds(r: r) {
    ExitSuccess
  } else {
    exit_failure(reason: concat("boot medium reader probe did not hold:\n", rendered))
  }
}

// A leg that was never dispatched, rendered as an answer so the fold judges one type. NOT presented as a
// refusal of the thing it stands for: the fold reaches it only on a path whose cause the stopping stage
// has already named.
fn unreadable_answer() -> RemoteAnswer {
  RemoteAnswer { exit_code: 255, stdout: "", stderr: "leg not dispatched: an earlier stage refused" }
}

// ONE LEG UNDER THE READER KEY, over the reader's own prepared context. The forced command makes argv
// advisory -- the server runs the forced command whatever is sent -- which is precisely the property under
// test, and precisely why the second command must travel this route and never the administrator one.
fn reader_leg(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, argv: List<String>) -> RemoteAnswer {
  remote_answer_of(outcome: typed_argv_exec_over_fleet_ssh(target: target, context: context, argv: argv))
}

// ONE LEG AS ROOT, the same elevation B2b's own legs use (gunbc.runner.runner_host_file_converge
// LegElevated is sudo_elevate over the same transport), so the two runs of the same read agree on how
// privilege is obtained and differ only in which read they ask for.
fn elevated_leg(context: FleetSshExecutionContext, target: SshTarget, command: List<String>) -> RemoteAnswer {
  let inv = sudo_elevate(command: command)
  remote_answer_of(outcome: typed_argv_exec_over_fleet_ssh(target: target, context: context, argv: append([inv.bin_path], items: inv.args)))
}

// OPEN FACT 2'S LEG: the readability question asked AS the reader, from the privileged route, because under
// the reader key every question is answered by the forced line. `test -r` on the served link is the
// question, and `sudo -u` is how it is asked without the key.
fn as_reader_leg(context: FleetSshExecutionContext, target: SshTarget) -> RemoteAnswer {
  let inv = sudo_elevate_as(user: boot_medium_reader_login, command: argv_words(command: test_readable_command(path: boot_medium_served_link_path() as String)))
  remote_answer_of(outcome: typed_argv_exec_over_fleet_ssh(target: target, context: context, argv: append([inv.bin_path], items: inv.args)))
}

// THE FORCED COMMAND'S OWN ARGV, taken from the one place the fleet spells that command
// (boot_medium_served_argv, the same builder the readback and the key's forced text both derive from), so
// the probe cannot ask for a digest whose spelling drifted from the one B2b installed. The server's
// authorized_keys ForceCommand is authoritative over anything sent here -- sending it is what lets a
// non-holding server be caught, not what makes it hold.
fn reader_probe_forced_argv() -> List<String> {
  boot_medium_served_argv(path: boot_medium_served_link_path())
}

// THE SECOND COMMAND. Read-only; prints `uid=<n> (<name>)`, which no sha256sum line can be confused with;
// and prints nothing secret even if the server ever ran it -- which is the case this exists to catch.
fn reader_probe_second_command() -> List<String> {
  id_user_name_argv()
}
